# XBD 4.16 Pathname Resolution
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The general rules, tested through stat(), lstat(), open(), access(),
# chdir(), the *at() calls and the creation calls, in a private directory.
# Function modules test the same rules for their own call (their trailing-
# slash, ELOOP and ENAMETOOLONG assertions). Where a name that is too long
# or lies below a file also names nothing, XSH 2.3 lets either error be
# reported (Q-8): stat() accepts both, the creation calls only the one that
# applies. Not tested: a pathname of exactly two leading slashes
# (implementation-defined), dot-dot at the root (may be the root itself),
# and the process root directory (changing it is not POSIX).

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       An absolute pathname starts at the root and ignores the working
           directory; a relative one starts at the working directory and
           follows chdir(); with fstatat() and openat() a relative one
           starts at the directory the descriptor names, not the working
           directory, and an absolute one ignores the descriptor; "/" is a
           directory and "/." is the same one.
test       t/001-absolute-relative.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       Resolution fails when a component is not in the directory
           before it: ENOENT for a missing name or directory (stat, open,
           access, chdir, unlink), ENOTDIR for chdir() or opendir() of a
           regular file, and a failure (ENOTDIR or ENOENT) for names below
           a regular file, including d/f/. and d/f/..
test       t/002-resolution-fails.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       To create an entry only its path prefix is resolved: new names
           below an existing directory can be made with open(O_CREAT),
           mkdir(), symlink(), mkfifo(), link() and rename(), while the
           same calls below a missing directory fail with ENOENT and make
           nothing; an existing last component is found (EEXIST).
test       t/003-resolution-for-creation.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.146
class      required
says       A name of {NAME_MAX} bytes (pathconf of the directory) can be
           created, found, listed and removed; a component of {NAME_MAX}+1
           bytes is an error: open(O_CREAT) and mkdir() fail with
           ENAMETOOLONG and create nothing (no truncated name), stat()
           fails with ENAMETOOLONG or ENOENT, at the end of a pathname and
           in the middle. NOTINUSE where {NAME_MAX} is unlimited.
note       The text makes it an error whatever the value of
           _POSIX_NO_TRUNC; the function pages give ENAMETOOLONG.
test       t/004-name-max.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       Trailing slashes: "d/", "d///" and "sd/" (a link to a directory)
           resolve to the directory (lstat("sd/") describes the directory
           and lstat("sd") the link); "f/" and "sf/" (a link to a file)
           fail with ENOTDIR in stat, lstat, open, access and chdir; a
           dangling link with a slash fails with ENOENT; mkdir("nd/") and
           mkdir("nd2//") make directories; open("newfile/", O_CREAT) makes
           nothing; "///" is the root.
test       t/005-trailing-slashes.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.006
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       A symbolic link in the middle of a pathname is followed, also by
           unlink(); at the end, stat() and open() follow links (also
           chains of links) while lstat(), readlink(), symlink() (EEXIST),
           rename(), unlink() and remove() act on the link itself and leave
           its target.
test       t/006-symlink-follow-or-act.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.007
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       Link contents take the place of the link's name: a relative link
           (also with ..) is read from the directory that holds the link,
           not the working directory, an absolute one from the root, chains
           are followed, and links containing only slashes ("/" and "///")
           make the rest of the pathname start at the root, however many
           slashes follow them.
test       t/007-symlink-contents.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.008
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       A symbolic link with empty contents resolves either to ENOENT or
           to the directory that contains the link (so "es" is "." and
           "es/f" is "./f"); lstat() of the link works. NOTINUSE where such
           a link cannot be made.
test       t/008-empty-symlink.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.009
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       A loop of links (a to b to a, a link to itself) fails with ELOOP
           in stat(), open() and access() (also with a name below it), while
           lstat() of a link in the loop works; a chain of {SYMLOOP_MAX}
           links (at least 8, at most 200 tried) is followed, below it and
           at its end.
test       t/009-loops-and-limit.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.010
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       Dot is the directory itself and dot-dot its parent: d/. is d,
           d/sub/.. is d, d/sub/../.. is the working directory, long chains
           of both resolve; dot-dot after a link to d/sub is the parent of
           d/sub, not the directory that holds the link; and from inside
           d/sub, .. and ../../d are as expected.
test       t/010-dot-and-dotdot.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.011
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       The empty pathname is never resolved: stat, lstat, open (also
           with O_CREAT), access, chdir, unlink, rmdir, mkdir, mkfifo,
           readlink, fstatat (also with a descriptor), opendir, and the
           first or second pathname of rename, link and symlink all fail
           with ENOENT, and nothing is created.
test       t/011-empty-pathname.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.012
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap03.html#3.254
class      required
says       Repeated slashes count as one, inside a pathname and in front
           of it when there are three or more; "////" and "/./" are the
           root; an absolute pathname with leading and inner slashes
           repeated names the same file.
test       t/012-slashes.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.013
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       For a fixed set of pathnames (existing, missing, below a file,
           with a trailing slash, through links, in a loop, dangling, empty,
           absolute, too long a component), stat(), access(), fstatat(),
           open() and statvfs() succeed or fail together and, where the
           error is not a matter of choice, with the same error.
test       t/013-same-result-everywhere.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.014
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       When a link's contents plus the rest of the pathname exceed
           {PATH_MAX}, stat() either fails with ENAMETOOLONG or resolves
           to the file the short pathname leads to (a link of nearly
           {PATH_MAX} bytes followed by 24 "./" components and f).
           NOTINUSE where {PATH_MAX} is unlimited or such a link cannot be
           made.
test       t/014-path-max-with-links.c

assertion  XBD.4.16-pathname-resolution.015
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.16
class      required
says       Different pathnames give the same directory entry and different
           entries the same file: spellings of a directory (with ., .., a
           trailing slash, a link, the absolute path, ../name/d) all give
           the same directory, and the spellings of a file (also through a
           link, and a second hard link) the same file.
test       t/015-aliases.c
